# Chromebook top-row keys (Reawakening OS)
#
# A Chromebook's keyboard has no Fn key: the top row *is* back, forward,
# refresh, full-screen, overview, brightness down/up, mute, volume down/up.
# Depending on the model and firmware, those keys reach Linux either as the
# XF86 media keysyms (in which case labwc's own binds already handle them) or
# as plain F6-F10, which is what makes Brave open its caret-browsing prompt on
# F7 instead of turning the screen down.
#
# This hwdb entry rewrites the kernel's key codes for the internal keyboard of
# Google hardware. The atkbd driver numbers the top row F6..F10 as scancodes
# 0x40..0x44; those five become the brightness and audio keys, so the keysyms
# the compositor receives are the same ones a normal laptop sends and no Fn key
# is needed. External keyboards and every other machine are untouched: the rule
# only matches an atkbd device whose DMI vendor is Google.
#
# If a particular model already delivers XF86 keysyms this rule never fires
# (its keyboard is not an atkbd device), so it cannot double-apply.
evdev:atkbd:dmi:*:svnGOOGLE*:pn*
 KEYBOARD_KEY_40=brightnessdown
 KEYBOARD_KEY_41=brightnessup
 KEYBOARD_KEY_42=mute
 KEYBOARD_KEY_43=volumedown
 KEYBOARD_KEY_44=volumeup

evdev:atkbd:dmi:*:svnGoogle*:pn*
 KEYBOARD_KEY_40=brightnessdown
 KEYBOARD_KEY_41=brightnessup
 KEYBOARD_KEY_42=mute
 KEYBOARD_KEY_43=volumedown
 KEYBOARD_KEY_44=volumeup
